
1. This script traps four signals, and a helper sends it three
--------------------------------------------------------------
trap 'echo "caught SIGINT  -- not stopping" >> log' INT
trap 'echo "caught SIGQUIT -- not stopping" >> log' QUIT
trap 'echo "caught SIGTERM -- would clean up here" >> log' TERM
trap 'echo "caught SIGKILL" >> log' KILL

bash accepted all four without a word. Sending the first three:

$ cat log
caught SIGINT  -- not stopping
caught SIGQUIT -- not stopping
caught SIGTERM -- would clean up here

Three signals, three lines, and this script is still the one printing.
Ctrl-C sends SIGINT and Ctrl-\ sends SIGQUIT, so at a terminal neither
key would have ended it either.

2. The fourth trap, which will never run
--------------------------------------------------------------
A child with the same 'trap ... KILL' line, then kill -KILL:

$ cat log
caught SIGINT  -- not stopping
caught SIGQUIT -- not stopping
caught SIGTERM -- would clean up here

$ wait "$victim"; echo $?
137

The log is unchanged -- still the three lines from section 1. SIGKILL is
never delivered to the process; the kernel acts on it instead, so there
is no handler to run and no last words to write. A shell that accepts
the trap is doing what POSIX allows: trapping it is undefined, not an
error, so nothing tells you.

3. What the number means
--------------------------------------------------------------
A process ended BY a signal reports 128 + that signal's number:

signal     number   status   where you meet it
SIGINT     2        130      Ctrl-C, or a build you interrupted
SIGQUIT    3        131      Ctrl-\, and a core dump if your ulimit allows one
SIGTERM    15       143      kill with no argument; what an init system sends first
SIGKILL    9        137      kill -9, and the out-of-memory killer

So 130 in a CI log is somebody's Ctrl-C, 137 is usually the
out-of-memory killer, and 143 is a shutdown that asked politely first.

4. kill -l, and the one number that is not portable
--------------------------------------------------------------
$ kill -l KILL
9

$ kill -l STOP
17

SIGKILL is 9 on every Unix in use. SIGSTOP is not -- macOS says 17 and
Linux says 19, which is the whole difference between the two columns on
this page. Name signals in a script; never number them.

5. The other one nothing can catch
--------------------------------------------------------------
SIGSTOP suspends a process and is equally uncatchable. The difference is
that it is reversible:

$ ps -o state= -p "$victim" | tr -d " " | cut -c1
T

$ ps -o state= -p "$victim" | tr -d " " | cut -c1
S

T is stopped, and the process still exists -- still holding its memory,
its open files and its place in the process table. Only SIGCONT starts
it again. That pair is what Ctrl-Z and fg are made of, which is why they
are not in a trap table.

6. The third way a trap never runs, and no kernel rule is involved
--------------------------------------------------------------
One script, run two ways. First in the background, from this
non-interactive shell, then sent a SIGINT:

$ cat log

Empty. A shell starting a background job sets the child's SIGINT and
SIGQUIT to ignored before it execs, and POSIX says a signal already
ignored when a shell starts cannot be trapped, reset or caught. bash
honours that: the trap command succeeds and does nothing.

The same trap worked in section 1, where this script was in front. And
it is bash being strict rather than the kernel being strict -- the same
child written in zsh or fish catches the signal, because neither honours
that rule. So a wrapper script can work when you test it by hand, work
under zsh, and go quiet the day it is backgrounded under bash.
